All 22 cases pass. Final implementation:

```js
function validatePassword(password) {
  if (typeof password !== "string") return false;
  return (
    password.length >= 8 &&
    /\d/.test(password) &&
    /[A-Z]/.test(password) &&
    /[^A-Za-z0-9\s]/.test(password)
  );
}
```

Notes on the design:
- **Special character** = any non-alphanumeric, non-whitespace character (`[^A-Za-z0-9\s]`). This avoids an arbitrary symbol allow-list that could wrongly reject `` ` `` or `~`. Whitespace alone does not count as special.
- Non-string inputs (number, null, undefined) return `false` rather than throwing, since `password.length`/regex `.test` on those would misbehave in a signup flow.
- Four independent checks, all required (length ≥ 8, has digit, has uppercase, has special).

Verified against 22 cases covering each rule and boundary conditions (0 failures). No test suite existed for this file, so I wrote ad-hoc assertions in a throwaway script — nothing was added to the repo.

